# Every text file is stored with LF whatever the committer's git does, so the tree is clean on a clone without core.autocrlf
# (NOTES-FROM-PLANNING.md entry 70 section 1). The rules below narrow it where bytes are compared exactly.
* text=auto

# Canonical GLTD-J is LF-only by specification (TARGET-SCHEMA.md section 6),
# and fixtures are compared byte for byte, so neither may be converted on checkout.
targets/** text eol=lf
tests/**/Fixtures/** text eol=lf
src/**/*.json text eol=lf
scans/phase0/measurements/** text eol=lf

# Generated measurement sheets are compared byte for byte, and a PDF is binary whatever its bytes look like.
scans/phase1/** text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
# NOTES-FROM-PLANNING.md entry 110: the ported JavaScript is kept byte for byte, so its recorded SHA-256 holds on any checkout, and the fixture
# its Node run produces is compared byte for byte in CI.
reference/ballistics-js/ballistics.js -text
tests/GroupLab.Core.Tests/Fixtures/ballistics-js.json -text

# NOTES-FROM-PLANNING.md entry 181: everything under website/server is copied to a Linux server straight from the working tree, and a
# Windows checkout left it CRLF, so the intake worker's shebang read "python3" and it would not start. LF in every working tree.
website/server/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

